<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="sec-directional-derivatives">
	<title>Gradient descent</title>

		<example xml:id="act-10-1-solve-equation">
      <title>Critical-point equations in three variables (U3-LO9)</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find the system of equations satisfied by the critical points of the function
					<me>f(x,y,z) = x^2e^{yz} + y\sin(xz) + z\cos(xy)</me>.
				</p>
			</statement>

			<solution>
				<p>
					We calculate that
					<me>
						f_x(x,y,z)=2xe^{yz}+yz\cos(xz)-yz\sin(xy)
					</me>,
					that
					<me>
						f_y(x,y,z)=x^2ze^{yz}+\sin(xz)-xz\sin(xy)
					</me>
					and that
					<me>
						f_z(x,y,z)=x^2ye^{yz}+xy\cos(xz)+\cos(xy)
					</me>.
					So the critical points of <m>f</m> are the solutions to the system of equations
					<me>
						\begin{cases}
						2xe^{yz}+yz\cos(xz)-yz\sin(xy)=0,\\
						x^2ze^{yz}+\sin(xz)-xz\sin(xy)=0,\\
						x^2ye^{yz}+xy\cos(xz)+\cos(xy)=0.
						\end{cases}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>

		<p>
			Using the calculus we have developed, it is usually straightforward to write the equations that critical points must satisfy. <xref ref="act-10-1-solve-equation"/> also shows the problem: those equations may be nonlinear and difficult or impossible to solve exactly. This is different from the linear systems in Unit 2, where Gaussian elimination gave a systematic exact method.
		</p>

	<p>
		When the critical-point equations cannot be solved directly, one often uses a numerical method. By <xref ref="thm-10-1-directional-derivative-extrema"/>, the negative gradient gives the direction of steepest local decrease.
	</p>

	<p>
		At a current point <m>\mathbf x_k</m>, choose
		<me>\mathbf h=-\alpha\nabla f(\mathbf x_k),\qquad \alpha>0.</me>
		By <xref ref="def-9-1-linear-approximation"/>,
		<md>
			<mrow>f(\mathbf x_k+\mathbf h)-f(\mathbf x_k) \amp\approx \nabla f(\mathbf x_k)^T\mathbf h</mrow>
			<mrow>\amp=-\alpha\|\nabla f(\mathbf x_k)\|^2.</mrow>
		</md>
		Unless the gradient is zero, the local model predicts a decrease. This is a local prediction, not a convergence theorem. It motivates the gradient-descent update
		<me>\mathbf x_{k+1}=\mathbf x_k-\alpha\nabla f(\mathbf x_k).</me>
	</p>

	<p>The gradient-descent algorithm repeats this local step.</p>

		<ol>
			<li>Start at an initial point <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m>.</li>
			<li>Compute the gradient <m>\nabla f(\mathbf{x}_0)</m>.</li>
			<li>Move in the direction of the negative gradient: set <m>\mathbf{x}_1 = \mathbf{x}_0 - \alpha_0 \nabla f(\mathbf{x}_0)</m>, where <m>\alpha_0 > 0</m> is a step size (or learning rate) parameter.</li>
			<li>Repeat steps 2-3 with step sizes <m>\alpha_1, \alpha_2, \dots</m> to successively define points <m>\mathbf{x}_2 = \mathbf{x}_1 - \alpha_1 \nabla f(\mathbf{x}_1)</m>, <m>\mathbf{x}_3 = \mathbf{x}_2 - \alpha_2 \nabla f(\mathbf{x}_2)</m>, and so on, with the hope that these points converge to a local minimum.</li>
		</ol>

		<p>
			In data and modeling problems, an objective function being minimized is often called a loss.
		</p>

		<p>
			Intuitively, gradient descent works because each successive value <m>\mathbf{x}_k</m> is obtained by shifting the previous value <m>\mathbf{x}_{k-1}</m> in the direction in which the function <m>f</m> decreases the fastest. Gradient descent may converge to a local minimum under additional assumptions on the function and the learning rates. The examples below illustrate possible behavior; they do not prove convergence.
		</p>

	<activity>
    <title>Three gradient-descent steps (U3-LO9)</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Find an approximate minimum of the function <m>f : \R^2 \to \R</m> defined by
				<me>f(x, y) = x^2 - 3xy + 3y^2 + 5y + 2x</me>
				using gradient descent. Use the starting point <m>\mathbf{x}_0 = (0,0)</m>, a constant step size of <m>0.1</m>, and stop after computing <m>\mathbf{x}_3</m>. You may use a calculator to perform these computations.
			</p>
		</statement>

		<solution>
			<p>
				First, we calculate the gradient:
				<me>
					\nabla f(x, y) = \begin{bmatrix} 2x - 3y + 2 \\ -3x + 6y + 5 \end{bmatrix}
				</me>
				Starting at <m>\mathbf{x}_0 = (0,0)</m>, we successively compute that
				<me>
					\nabla f(0,0) = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix}
				</me>,
				so
				<me>
					\mathbf{x}_1  = \begin{bmatrix}0\\0\end{bmatrix} - 0.1\begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 5 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -0.2 \\ -0.5 \end{bmatrix}
				</me>.
				We then compute that
				<md>
                <mrow>\amp\nabla f(-0.2,-0.5)</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp=\begin{bmatrix}-0.4-(-1.5)+2\\-3(-0.2)+6(-0.5)+5\end{bmatrix}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp=\begin{bmatrix}3.1\\2.6\end{bmatrix}</mrow>
              </md>,
				so
				<me>
					\mathbf{x}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} -0.2 \\ -0.5 \end{bmatrix} - 0.1\begin{bmatrix} 3.1 \\ 2.6 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -0.51 \\ -0.76 \end{bmatrix}
				</me>.
				Finally, we compute that
				<md>
                <mrow>\amp\nabla f(-0.51,-0.76)</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp=\begin{bmatrix}2(-0.51)-3(-0.76)+2\\-3(-0.51)+6(-0.76)+5\end{bmatrix}</mrow>
                <mrow>\amp=\begin{bmatrix}3.26\\1.97\end{bmatrix}</mrow>
              </md>,
				so
				<me>
					\mathbf{x}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} -0.51 \\ -0.76 \end{bmatrix} - 0.1\begin{bmatrix} 3.26 \\ 1.97 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} -0.836 \\ -0.957 \end{bmatrix}
				</me>.
					In <xref ref="act-u5-classify-gradient-descent-quadratic"/>, we compute exactly that the unique critical point is
					<me>\left(-9,-\frac{16}{3}\right)</me>
					and classify it after developing second-order tools. Thus <m>\mathbf x_3</m> is not yet a good approximation. If we continue the iteration, the points <m>\mathbf x_k</m> for, say, <m>k\geq1000</m> are very close to that critical point.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</activity>

	<figure>
		<caption>Initial steps of gradient descent for <m>f(x,y)=x^2-3xy+3y^2+5y+2x</m> with starting point <m>\mathbf{x}_0 = (0, 0)</m>. For <m>k=1,2,3</m>, the point <m>\mathbf{x}_k</m> is determined by the rule <m>\mathbf{x}_k = \mathbf{x}_{k-1} - (0.1) \nabla f(\mathbf{x}_{k-1})</m>. Adapted from Stanford's MATH 51 textbook.</caption>
		<image source="figures/unit5/u5-gradient-descent-quadratic.png" width="80%">
			<shortdescription>
				Surface plot showing the first gradient descent steps from the origin.
			</shortdescription>
			<description>
				<p>
					The figure shows a side view of the surface <m>z=f(x,y)</m> for <m>f(x,y)=x^2-3xy+3y^2+5y+2x</m> over a small region near the origin. Four red points labeled <m>\mathbf{x}_0</m>, <m>\mathbf{x}_1</m>, <m>\mathbf{x}_2</m>, and <m>\mathbf{x}_3</m> are connected by red line segments on the surface, showing the first three gradient descent steps from <m>(0,0)</m>.
				</p>
			</description>
		</image>
	</figure>

    <activity xml:id="act-u3-sympy-gradient-descent-step">
      <title>From a symbolic gradient to a descent step (U3-LO9)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use SymPy to check the first step of the preceding calculation for <m>f(x,y)=x^2-3xy+3y^2+5y+2x</m>. We construct a gradient as in <xref ref="act-u3-sympy-gradient-hessian"/>, evaluate it at the starting point, and apply the gradient-descent update. The learning rate <c>sp.Rational(1, 10)</c> represents <m>0.1</m> exactly.
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import sympy as sp

x, y = sp.symbols("x y")
f = x**2 - 3*x*y + 3*y**2 + 5*y + 2*x
grad_f = sp.Matrix([sp.diff(f, x), sp.diff(f, y)])

p0 = sp.Matrix([0, 0])
g0 = grad_f.subs({x: p0[0], y: p0[1]})
alpha = sp.Rational(1, 10)
p1 = p0 - alpha*g0
f0 = f.subs({x: p0[0], y: p0[1]})
f1 = f.subs({x: p1[0], y: p1[1]})

print(grad_f)
print(g0)
print(p1)
print(f0, f1)
print((g0.T * (p1 - p0))[0], f1 - f0)
]]></code></program>
        <p>Output:</p>
        <program language="text"><code><![CDATA[Matrix([[2*x - 3*y + 2], [-3*x + 6*y + 5]])
Matrix([[2], [5]])
Matrix([[-1/5], [-1/2]])
0 -241/100
-29/10 -241/100
]]></code></program>
        <p>
          In the last line, <c>g0.T * (p1 - p0)</c> is a <m>1\times1</m> matrix. The index <c>[0]</c> extracts its scalar entry, the local linear prediction for the change in <m>f</m>.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>What is the difference between <c>grad_f</c> and <c>g0</c>? What shapes must <c>p0</c>, <c>g0</c>, and <c>p1</c> have for the update to make sense?</p></li>
          <li><p>Write the numerical vector update that produces <c>p1</c>. Did the function value decrease? Compare the predicted change with the actual change, using the scalar linear approximation from <xref ref="def-9-1-linear-approximation"/>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Predict <c>grad_f.subs({x: p1[0], y: p1[1]})</c>. Is <c>p1</c> a critical point? Explain which symbolic expression can be reused at the next step and which evaluated vector must be recomputed.</p></li>
          <li><p>If <c>alpha</c> is changed to <c>1</c> and the subsequent lines are rerun, what are <c>p1</c> and <c>f1</c>? What does this comparison show about the learning rate?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The matrix <c>grad_f</c> contains expressions for the two partial derivatives as functions of <m>x,y</m>; <c>g0</c> is their value at <m>(0,0)</m>. Each point and evaluated gradient is a <m>2\times1</m> column, so the scalar multiple <c>alpha*g0</c> can be subtracted from <c>p0</c>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The update is
              <me>
                \mathbf{p}_1
                =\begin{bmatrix}0\\0\end{bmatrix}
                -\frac{1}{10}\begin{bmatrix}2\\5\end{bmatrix}
                =\begin{bmatrix}-1/5\\-1/2\end{bmatrix}.
              </me>
              The function value decreases from <m>0</m> to <m>-241/100=-2.41</m>. The local linear approximation predicts
              <me>
                \nabla f(\mathbf{p}_0)^T(\mathbf{p}_1-\mathbf{p}_0)
                =-\frac{1}{10}(2^2+5^2)
                =-\frac{29}{10}.
              </me>
              The actual change is <m>-241/100</m>. Both are negative, but the actual decrease is smaller than predicted because the linear approximation omits nonlinear terms.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Evaluating at the new point gives
              <me>
                \nabla f(-1/5,-1/2)
                =\begin{bmatrix}31/10\\13/5\end{bmatrix},
              </me>
              so <m>\mathbf{p}_1</m> is not a critical point. The symbolic expression <c>grad_f</c> can be reused without differentiating again. Its value must be recomputed at the new point; reusing the old vector <c>g0</c> would not implement gradient descent at that point.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              With <m>\alpha=1</m>, the new point is <m>(-2,-5)</m> and <m>f(-2,-5)=20</m>, an increase from <m>0</m>. The local model predicts a change of <m>-29</m>, but this larger step goes too far for that prediction to ensure a decrease. A negative-gradient direction does not guarantee a decrease for every positive learning rate.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    </activity>

	<remark>
		<statement>
			<p>
				The iteration is cumbersome by hand but easy to repeat on a computer when the gradient is available.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</remark>

    <activity xml:id="act-u3-prediction-loss-descent">
      <title>Improving a model's predictions with one step (U3-LO8, U3-LO9)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Return to the three observations from <xref ref="act-u3-prediction-loss-model"/> and the loss
          <me>\mathcal L(w_1,w_2)=(w_1-1)^2+(w_2-2)^2+(w_1+w_2-2)^2.</me>
          At the starting weights <m>\mathbf{w}_0=(1,1)</m>, the loss is <m>1</m> and the gradient is <m>(0,-2)</m>. In this model, a training step adjusts the weights while the features <m>X</m> and observations <m>\mathbf{y}</m> remain fixed.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Find the unit direction of greatest decrease at <m>\mathbf{w}_0</m> and its directional derivative. Take one gradient-descent step with learning rate <m>\alpha=1/10</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute the new prediction vector, residual, and loss. Compare the actual change with the first-order prediction <m>-\alpha\|\nabla\mathcal L(\mathbf{w}_0)\|^2</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Evaluate the gradient at the new weights. Has the step reached a critical point?</p></li>
          <li><p>Starting again at <m>\mathbf{w}_0</m>, what happens if <m>\alpha=1</m>? Explain why the same descent direction can give an increase in loss.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The negative gradient is <m>(0,2)</m>, so its unit direction is <m>(0,1)</m>. The directional derivative is <m>(0,-2)\cdot(0,1)=-2</m>. Gradient descent uses the unnormalized gradient:
              <me>
                \mathbf{w}_1
                =\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}
                -\frac{1}{10}\begin{bmatrix}0\\-2\end{bmatrix}
                =\begin{bmatrix}1\\6/5\end{bmatrix}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The new predictions and residuals are
              <me>
                X\mathbf{w}_1=\begin{bmatrix}1\\6/5\\11/5\end{bmatrix},
                \qquad R(\mathbf{w}_1)=\begin{bmatrix}0\\-4/5\\1/5\end{bmatrix}.
              </me>
              The loss is <m>16/25+1/25=17/25=0.68</m>, down from <m>1</m>. The predicted change is <m>-(1/10)(4)=-2/5</m>, while the actual change is <m>-8/25</m>. Their difference, <m>2/25</m>, is the squared length of <m>X(\mathbf{w}_1-\mathbf{w}_0)=(0,1/5,1/5)</m>, as in <xref ref="act-u3-prediction-loss-linearization"/>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The gradient formula from <xref ref="act-u3-prediction-loss-chain-rule"/> gives
              <me>
                \nabla\mathcal L(\mathbf{w}_1)
                =2X^T\begin{bmatrix}0\\-4/5\\1/5\end{bmatrix}
                =\begin{bmatrix}2/5\\-6/5\end{bmatrix}.
              </me>
              It is nonzero, so the new weights are not a critical point. The step improves the fit without finishing the minimization.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              With <m>\alpha=1</m>, the new weights are <m>(1,3)</m>, the predictions are <m>(1,3,4)</m>, and the residual is <m>(0,1,2)</m>. The loss becomes <m>5</m>, which is larger than the starting value. The negative gradient specifies a direction of local decrease; the learning rate determines how far we travel. A step that is too large can increase the loss, just as in <xref ref="act-u3-sympy-gradient-descent-step"/>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <p xml:id="p-u3-prediction-loss-unit4">
      These examples use derivatives to improve a fit and interpret directions that leave it unchanged. <xref ref="u4-orthogonality-least-squares" text="custom">Unit 4</xref> develops the general least-squares method for finding a best-fitting prediction vector and its geometric interpretation.
    </p>

	</subsection>
